  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the Midwest will slide east tonight\nand become centered overhead during the day Thursday. Low\npressure strengthening in the Canadian Maritimes will tighten\nthe gradient over the area leading to breezy NW winds overnight into\nThursday morning. As the high crests over the area Thursday, \nwinds will relax and become light and variable Thursday \nafternoon. Skies will be partly to mostly sunny with highs \nranging from the upper 40s north to near 60F south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure retreats to the east Thursday night with clouds\nincreasing as the next low pressure system approaches from the\nGreat Lakes. This low will continue to strengthen as it tracks\ntowards the St Lawrence Valley Friday and into the Canadian\nMaritimes Saturday night. Precipitation will break out from west\nto east early Friday morning with widespread rainfall expected\nthrough out the day Friday. Thermal profiles may be cold enough\nfor brief period of wintry mix at the highest elevations Friday\nmorning. Latest forecast rainfall amounts will range from 0.5\ninches south to 0.75 inches across the north with locally higher\namounts in the mountains. Steady precipitation will transition\nto showers Friday night with cold air moving in allowing for a \ntransition to snow showers in the mountains. As temperatures \ncontinue to cool into Saturday morning, snow will likely make \nit to the valley floors across the north. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe low pressure that passes through late this week deepens \nacross the Canadian Maritimes by Saturday. As high pressure \nbuilds in from the west, a strong pressure gradient brings gusty\nconditions, as well as a seasonably cold airmass for mid \nOctober.\n\nWind gusts on Saturday will mainly be around 30-35 mph, with \nsome gusts to around 40 mph. This will bring the chance for a \nfew downed branches and isolated power outages as one of the \nfirst windier days of the season knocks off some weaker \nbranches. Winds ease Saturday night, with lighter winds expected\nfor Sunday.\n\nTemperatures will be seasonably cold, especially for a day with\na mix of sun and clouds. Highs mainly hold in the 30s across \nthe north on Saturday, while the coastline struggles to reach 50\ndegrees. Scattered upslope snow showers are expected across the\nhigher terrain. Rising modeled froude numbers indicate that \nsome flurries and snow showers will make progress downwind of \nthe mountains Saturday afternoon, only gradually drying up as \nthey move southeastward. Lows will then be cold Saturday night, \nwith widespread temperatures in the 20s, and below freezing \ntemperatures expected all the way to the coastline.\n\nAcross the higher terrain, wintertime conditions are expected. \nAccumulating snowfall across the upslope terrain, temperatures \nin the teens, and wind chills well below zero are all expected. \nWind gusts over 100 mph are expected on Mount Washington on \nSaturday. Any hikers or those above 2500ft should plan for \nwintertime conditions this weekend.\n\nBy Sunday, winds ease noticeably, and temperatures begin to \nrebound by a few degrees. Highs are expected to range from the \nlow 40s across the north, to mid 50s along the coast by Sunday. \nThis moderating trend continues into early next week, with high \nrebounding into the 60s in most areas for Monday and Tuesday. \nHigh pressure remains in control of the weather pattern for at \nleast early to midweek next week, but models are indicating that\nlow pressure likely begins to organize to our south during this\ntimeframe, and will be worth monitoring through the forecast \nperiod.",
